# CYD Cross-Platform Font Unification [SPEC:CXC-CYD-FONT-UNIFY]

> The desktop Skia renderer and the CYD firmware draw the same scene, and text is where they diverge
> most: a measured maximum pixel delta of 200 in text areas. The cause is that the two tiers use two
> different text engines — Skia's native typeface rasteriser on the desktop, a pre-rendered bitmap atlas
> on the ESP32. This spec removes the divergence by removing the second engine: the desktop adopts the
> CYD's atlas. The direction is deliberate and one-way. The CONSTRAINED tier's renderer is the
> reference, and the capable tier moves to meet it, because only the constrained tier has no choice.
> The load-bearing consequence is that "pixel-identical across tiers" becomes checkable by a byte
> comparison instead of a tolerance.

---

## 1. Atlas export

### [CXC-CYD-FONT-EXPORT] One generator run emits both the C header and a Python-readable atlas

- file: cyd/fonts/generate_atlas.py
- requirement: produce TWO outputs from a single invocation — the existing C header for the firmware, and
  a new JSON atlas for the desktop.
- output-c: `cyd/src/font_atlas.h` — static arrays and struct initialisers, the existing firmware format.
- output-json: `python/composeio/render/font_atlas_data.json` — the same glyph data with hex-encoded bitmaps.
- rationale: a single generator run is what makes the two tiers provably the same data. Two generators,
  or one generator run twice, reintroduce exactly the skew this spec exists to remove.
- requirement-every-family: EVERY family/weight group the run emits to the C header MUST also be emitted to
  the JSON. "Both outputs" is not "both files exist" — it is "both files carry the same family set". A run
  that writes a group to one output and not the other produces two files with one timestamp and two
  contents, which defeats the single-run guarantee more quietly than two runs would.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CYD-FONT-UNIFY]

### [CXC-CYD-FONT-EXPORT-SCHEMA] The JSON atlas schema, and the hex bitmap encoding

- definition: a top-level object carrying generator provenance plus a `sizes` array, one entry per atlas
  size, each holding per-size metrics and a `glyphs` array.
- provenance-keys: `generator`, `font_source` (the TTF path actually used), `dpi`, `char_range`
- size-keys: `size`, `ascent`, `descent`, `line_height`, `glyphs`
- glyph-keys: `char_code`, `width`, `height`, `bearing_x`, `bearing_y`, `advance`, `bitmap_hex`
- encoding: `bitmap_hex` is the glyph's 8-bit alpha bitmap as a contiguous hex string — two hex
  characters per byte, row-major, exactly `width * height` bytes.
- empty-glyph: a glyph with no coverage (the space character) carries `bitmap_hex` as the empty string,
  not a run of zeroes.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CYD-FONT-UNIFY]

### [CXC-CYD-FONT-EXPORT-REGEN] Regeneration is atomic across both outputs

- requirement: running `generate_atlas.py` regenerates BOTH outputs; the `--sizes` flag applies to both.
- default-sizes: `8 10 12 14 16`, matching the CYD atlas at the time of writing.
- constraint: the two outputs can never be regenerated independently. A C header and a JSON atlas built
  from different runs would satisfy every test in this spec and still diverge on the glass.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CYD-FONT-UNIFY]

---

## 2. The desktop bitmap font renderer

### [CXC-CYD-FONT-RENDERER-CLASS] BitmapFontAtlas — glyph lookup and text measurement

- file: python/composeio/render/bitmap_font.py
- definition: a new module holding the desktop-side atlas reader; it loads the JSON atlas and provides
  glyph lookup plus text measurement.
- api: `__init__(json_path)`; `find_atlas(target_size) -> FontSizeData`;
  `measure_text(text, font_size) -> TextMetrics`; `get_glyph(char_code, atlas) -> GlyphData | None`
- constraint: `measure_text` MUST reproduce `ESP32Canvas::measure_text` exactly — find the best atlas
  size, scale by `font_size / atlas.size`, and sum `advance * scale` for the width.
- consequence: measurement is what drives alignment and layout. A measurement that disagrees with the
  firmware misplaces text even when every glyph is byte-identical, which is the harder failure to see.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CYD-FONT-UNIFY]

### [CXC-CYD-FONT-RENDERER-INTEGRATE] Atlas mode is an explicit opt-in on SkiaCanvas

- file: python/composeio/render/skia_backend.py
- api: `enable_bitmap_atlas(atlas_path: str) -> None` — switches text rendering to bitmap atlas mode.
- behaviour-set-font: `set_font(family, size, weight)` records `size` for atlas lookup, `weight` for the
  bold group, and `family` for the family group — see [CXC-CYD-FONT-FAMILY-REGISTRY].
- drift-note: v1.1 of this node said `family` and `weight` "are ignored because the atlas carries one
  family and one weight". Both clauses are now FALSE and were false in the code before they were false in
  the spec: [SPEC:CXC-CYD-BOLD-ATLAS] added the bold group, and the COMMP ("Common Pixel") family was added
  to `generate_atlas.py` + `cyd/src/font_atlas.h` on the firmware side without ever reaching this spec or
  the desktop. A reimplementation built from the old text would have re-dropped both — which is precisely
  what happened to COMMP on the desktop tier.
- behaviour-measure: `measure_text(text)` delegates to `BitmapFontAtlas.measure_text()`.
- behaviour-draw: `draw_text(text, x, y, align)` decodes each glyph's hex bitmap to an alpha array,
  creates a single-channel Skia image, and draws it with the fill colour modulated by alpha. Alignment
  logic is unchanged from the native path.
- constraint: with atlas mode DISABLED, `SkiaCanvas` must behave exactly as before. This is what keeps
  the change reversible and lets the crosscheck render both ways for comparison.
- supersedes-note: the `weight` argument stops being ignorable once [SPEC:CXC-CYD-BOLD-ATLAS] adds a
  bold weight group; that spec is what makes it load-bearing again.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CYD-FONT-UNIFY]

### [CXC-CYD-FONT-RENDERER-BLIT] Glyph blitting must use the firmware's integer arithmetic

- definition: the per-glyph blit sequence inside `draw_text`, which is the critical path for pixel
  fidelity and the place where a plausible-looking floating-point rewrite silently breaks equivalence.
- step-lookup: look up the glyph in the atlas using the same `font_atlas_find` / `font_atlas_glyph`
  logic as the firmware.
- step-scale: compute `scale = font_size / atlas.size`.
- step-position: `gx = draw_x + bearing_x * scale`; `gy = draw_y + (ascent - bearing_y) * scale`.
- step-resample: scale the glyph bitmap by NEAREST NEIGHBOUR, matching the firmware's
  `src_row = py * glyph_h / sh` integer expression.
- step-blend: for each non-zero alpha pixel, blend `fill_color` at `alpha * fill_alpha / 255`.
- reference: `cyd/src/canvas_esp32.cpp` lines 1408-1431 are the normative implementation.
- constraint: the scaling and blending must use the SAME INTEGER ARITHMETIC as the firmware. Integer
  truncation is part of the contract here, not an artefact to be improved away.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CYD-FONT-UNIFY]

---

## 2b. Family routing — the named family is part of the semantic value

> `[CXC-VISION-SEMANTIC-REALIZATION]` says one semantic value has many physical realizations. A scene's
> `font_family:` is part of that semantic value, not a rendering hint. If the tiers disagree about what a
> family NAME denotes, the same scene is two different scenes and the claim is false on the typography
> axis. Nothing in v1.1 of this spec said what a family name means, which is why the two tiers grew
> different answers without either one being wrong against the text.

### [CXC-CYD-FONT-FAMILY-REGISTRY] There is ONE declared family set, and every tier realizes all of it

- definition: the platform declares a CLOSED set of font family names. Today: `sans-serif` (IBM Plex Sans,
  regular/medium/bold), `monospace` (IBM Plex Mono), `Tiny5` (Colonial-OS pixel font),
  `commp` / `Common Pixel` (the COMMP pixel font).
- requirement: the family set is declared ONCE and consumed by reference. The atlas generator's family
  list, the firmware's `font_family_is_*` selectors, and the desktop's family routing table are three
  READERS of one declaration — never three hand-maintained enumerations.
- anti-pattern: `python/composeio/render/fonts.py::classify_family` and the generated
  `font_atlas.h::font_family_is_commp` were exactly such a pair. `Tiny5` was hand-added to the desktop
  table in a819a5f; `commp` was hand-added to the firmware generator and never to the desktop table. Same
  fork, two families, opposite directions. The second one is the defect this node exists to close.
- consequence: adding a family is one edit to the declaration plus a per-tier realization; a tier that
  cannot yet realize a declared family says so out loud ([CXC-CYD-FONT-FAMILY-NO-SILENT-FALLBACK]).
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CYD-FONT-UNIFY]

### [CXC-CYD-FONT-FAMILY-ONE-DIRECTORY] Font FILES live in one directory, and it is `<repo>/fonts/`

- requirement: every font file the platform declares lives in `<repo>/fonts/` — the one directory
  `composeio.render.fonts.resolve_font_path` searches — and every consumer reads it FROM THERE. The
  CYD atlas generator is a consumer, not an owner.
- rationale: `COMMP___.TTF` sat in `cyd/fonts/` — the FIRMWARE BUILD directory — next to
  `generate_atlas.py`, which consumed it. The desktop font resolver had no reason to look there, so the
  font was PRESENT IN THE REPOSITORY the entire time and the desktop still could not draw it. "Where do
  fonts live" had two answers, which is the same fork as "what does a family name mean" wearing
  different clothes.
- correction-note (2026-08-16): the first pass at this defect declared `commp` UNREALIZABLE on the
  desktop on the claim the TTF was absent from the filesystem. **That claim was false.** It came from a
  `find /` that was killed at a timeout, whose empty output was read as an empty result set — a SEARCH
  FAILURE reported as a fact about the world. The file was at `cyd/fonts/COMMP___.TTF`. The defect was
  never absence; it was DISTRIBUTION.
- verified-same-source: `fonts/COMMP___.TTF` is the file the compiled firmware atlas was generated
  from — 475 glyph metrics across sizes 12/14/16/20/24 and 435 glyph BITMAPS compared byte-for-byte
  against `cyd/src/font_atlas.h`, zero mismatches. Desktop FreeType and device atlas therefore draw
  COMMP from ONE source, which is what makes the parity claim checkable rather than hopeful.
- open-gap-distribution: `bus://assets/fonts/` serves only the five IBM Plex faces + the OFL licence.
  **Neither COMMP nor Tiny5 is on the bus.** `resolve_font_path`'s 4th step (fetch from
  `bus://assets/fonts/`) therefore cannot resolve either one, so a DEPLOYED surface with no repo
  checkout still renders the wrong face — silently before this spec, reported after it. Publishing both
  TTFs to bus_storage is the remaining work; it is a live shared service and belongs to the architect.
- open-gap-licence: `compose_projects/bsg/assets/manifest.md` flags COMMP with "verify redistribution
  licence before OSS release", and unlike Plex (`LICENSE-OFL.txt`) and Tiny5 (`Tiny5-OFL.txt`) it ships
  with no licence file. Moving it into `fonts/` puts it beside two OFL faces, which makes settling this
  more urgent, not less.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CYD-FONT-UNIFY]

### [CXC-CYD-FONT-EXPORT-TOOLCHAIN] Regeneration is only equivalent on the toolchain that produced the outputs

- premise: [CXC-CYD-FONT-EXPORT-REGEN] forbids regenerating the two outputs INDEPENDENTLY. It does not
  say — and it must — that regenerating them TOGETHER on a different FreeType produces a different atlas.
- measured-2026-08-16: re-running `generate_atlas.py` on the current dev machine and diffing glyph
  bitmaps against the committed `cyd/src/font_atlas.h`:
  **COMMP (pixel font, own hints, >=12px): 435 bitmaps, 0 differ. IBM Plex (antialiased, 6..16px): 658
  bitmaps, 658 differ — 100%.**
- consequence: "just regenerate the atlas" silently rewrites every Plex text pixel on every device. The
  COMMP family is grid-exact and toolchain-stable; the antialiased Plex glyphs are not.
- constraint: an atlas regeneration is a REVIEWED artifact change, pinned to a recorded FreeType
  version — never a build step someone runs to close a test.
- consequence-for-this-defect: `font_atlas_data.json` still carries no `commp` group. It cannot be
  closed here, because regenerating to add it would rewrite the Plex glyphs too. The desktop's LIVE text
  path is FreeType (`ft_text`), which now reads the COMMP TTF directly, so the reported defect is fixed;
  the ATLAS path (crosscheck + conformance) remains COMMP-less until a pinned-toolchain rebuild.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CYD-FONT-UNIFY]

### [CXC-CYD-FONT-FAMILY-DEFAULT] An omitted `font_family:` resolves to `sans-serif` on EVERY tier

- requirement: a scene element that carries `font_size:` but no `font_family:` renders in `sans-serif`.
  This is the resolved default at the CONTRACT level, not a per-backend fallback.
- current-implementations: `python/composeio/render/engine/parser.py` (`spec.get("font_family",
  "sans-serif")`) and `cyd/src/compose_scene.cpp` (`gets(s, spec, "font_family", "sans-serif")`) already
  AGREE. This node ratifies that agreement so a reimplementation of either side cannot silently pick a
  different default — the invariant lived only in two source lines and nothing tested that they matched.
- consequence: omitting `font_family:` is legal authoring and is NOT a parity defect. 64 of 140 text
  elements across the scene corpus omit it (measured 2026-08-16); every one of them is well-defined.
- non-consequence: this node does NOT make omission stylistically advisable. A scene that names a family
  on some elements and omits it on others gets TWO families on a tier that realizes both and ONE family on
  a tier that realizes neither — legible on both, identical on neither. That is an authoring finding, not
  a contract violation.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CYD-FONT-UNIFY]

### [CXC-CYD-FONT-FAMILY-NO-SILENT-FALLBACK] A family a tier cannot realize is REPORTED, never substituted

- requirement: when a scene names a family the running tier cannot realize, the tier MUST report it
  (a diagnostic the harness and the lint can read) before falling back. Rendering must not stop — a
  panel that goes blank is worse than a panel in the wrong face — but the substitution must be VISIBLE
  in a machine-readable place.
- rationale: this is the whole failure mode. `commp` on the desktop resolved through
  `classify_family("commp") -> "sans"` to `IBMPlexSans-Regular.ttf`, measured to the SAME advance width as
  `sans-serif` (129.0 px for "ENGINE THRUST" at 16px bold — byte-identical, not merely similar). The text
  drew, no error was raised, no log line appeared, and the scene was wrong on the glass. A silent
  substitution is indistinguishable from a correct render to every machine signal there is.
  AFTER the fix the same measurement reads 142.0 px for `commp` against 129.0 px for `sans-serif` — two
  faces, which is what the device has drawn all along.
- constraint: `classify_family` returning a best-effort class is fine; returning it WITHOUT recording that
  the name was unknown is what this node forbids.
- verify: an unknown/unrealizable family name appears in the tier's unresolved-family report; a declared
  and realizable one does not.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CYD-FONT-UNIFY]

